 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "Aggregate deposits with scheduled commercial banks is the aggregation of demand and time deposits of branches in and outside India. Demand deposits are liabilities payable on demand (current deposits, the demand-liability portion of savings deposits, credit balances in overdrafts and cash credit), while time deposits are liabilities payable otherwise than on demand (fixed deposits, cash certificates, cumulative and recurring deposits, the time-liability portion of savings deposits). RBI's CRR/SLR Master Direction states that 'aggregate deposits' means the aggregation of demand and time deposits. It is compiled from the fortnightly Form A return of SCBs under Section 42(2) of the RBI Act, 1934, and published as item 2.1 'Aggregate Deposits' in the WSS/RBI Bulletin.",
   "source_name": "RBI Master Direction — Cash Reserve Ratio and Statutory Liquidity Ratio Directions, 2021 (definition of aggregate deposits)",
   "source_url": "https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewMasDirections.aspx?id=12131",
   "term": "Aggregate deposits with scheduled commercial banks"
